# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
#
# NOTE the collision, which has already cost this repo one broken Windows build:
# `/build` is create-next-app boilerplate for an output directory this project
# never produces, but electron-builder's `directories.buildResources` is ALSO
# `build/` and holds the app icon, which MUST be committed. Ignoring the whole
# directory is how `win.icon: build/icon.ico` came to point at a file that was
# never in the repo — the config referenced it, every local build found it on
# disk, and a clean clone could not build at all. Keep the icon sources opted in.
# `/build/*`, NOT `/build`: git does not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so
# a negation under one can never re-include anything. Excluding the contents
# instead leaves the directory itself visible and makes the negations bite.
/build/*
!/build/icon.png
!/build/icon.svg

# misc
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files. Blanket-ignored: this app never NEEDS a .env, so anything matching
# is either a local path override or a mistake, and the mistake is the expensive
# case. The template is the one deliberate exception — without the negation,
# `.env*` swallows it and every clone loses the documentation.
.env*
!.env.example

# Credentials and signing material. None of this belongs in the repo, and each
# line is here because the file type is one an installer/build step drops into
# the working tree where `git add -A` would sweep it up.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.mobileprovision
*.jks
*.keystore
.npmrc
secrets.json

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# mobile single-file build artifact (rebuildable)
/mobile/dist

# electron-builder output (`npm run dist`). Rebuildable, and large — the Linux
# AppImage alone is ~220MB, so an accidental `git add -A` would try to commit it.
/dist-electron

# graphify graph data (regenerable)
/graphify-out

# Local scratch: model caches or exports someone points at the repo dir.
/tts-models
/stt-models

# root-level scratch dumps of the question banks (`_new-manifest.json`,
# `_cite-manifest.json`, …). Derived from data/certs/ to hand bank slices to audit
# agents, so they rot out of sync the moment the banks change — two were committed by
# a careless `git add -A`. Root-anchored and `_`-prefixed on purpose: it must not
# swallow real files in subdirectories or any file that isn't opted in by that prefix.
/_*.json
